    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-16 lg:py-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">

        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic border-l-4 border-[#38BDF8] pl-6 bg-gray-50 py-4 rounded-r-xl">
          Silicone is the better sealant and latex is the better filler, and most caulking failures come from asking one of them to do the other's job on the wrong kind of joint. The two tubes sit side by side and cost about the same, which is exactly why so many people reach for whichever one happens to be nearest.
        </p>

        <h2 id="the-paint-question" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          1. The Question That Decides It
        </h2>

        <p>
          Before comparing anything else, answer this: <strong>will this joint be painted?</strong>
        </p>

        <p>
          If yes, you are using acrylic latex or a paintable hybrid. Nothing adheres to cured silicone — not primer, not the best exterior paint on the market. Paint applied over silicone beads up while you are applying it, and whatever does cling peels within weeks. There is no technique that gets around this, and no amount of surface preparation changes it.
        </p>

        <p>
          If no, silicone is almost always the better choice, because everything else about it outperforms latex: it stays flexible for decades, it bonds to glass, tile, metal and plastic, and it is genuinely waterproof rather than merely water-resistant.
        </p>

        <p>
          That single fork resolves the majority of real decisions. The rest of this page is about the cases where it does not — high-movement joints, wet areas, mixed substrates, and the hybrid products that muddy the line deliberately.
        </p>

        <!-- Head-to-Head Comparison Table -->
