          </div>
        </div>

        <h2 id="silicone" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          3. What Silicone Does Well
        </h2>

        <p>
          <strong>It stays flexible permanently.</strong> Silicone cures to a rubber that keeps its elasticity for the life of the joint, which is why it survives in places that move — around windows that expand and contract through the seasons, where dissimilar materials meet, and at the perimeter of a bath that flexes when someone gets into it.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>It bonds to slick surfaces.</strong> Glass, glazed tile, porcelain, metal, acrylic and most plastics are exactly where latex fails, and exactly where silicone excels. A shower surround is a wall of non-porous material, which is why the answer there is not really debatable.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>It is genuinely waterproof.</strong> Not water-resistant once fully cured, but waterproof — it does not absorb water, soften, or break down in constant wet.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>It lasts.</strong> A well-applied silicone bead outliving two rounds of latex is normal, and on an unpainted exterior joint that is the whole argument.
        </p>

        <p>
          The costs are real, though. It cannot be painted, ever. It is fussier to tool, and the smear you leave on a surface has to be cleaned with solvent rather than a damp finger. Most importantly, <strong>nothing bonds to cured silicone</strong> — including fresh silicone — so a joint that has been siliconed once must be cut out and cleaned back to bare material before it can be redone. That single property makes it a commitment rather than just a product choice.
        </p>

        <h2 id="latex" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          4. What Latex Does Well
        </h2>

        <p>
          <strong>It takes paint perfectly.</strong> This is its reason to exist. Interior trim, baseboards, crown moulding, painted exterior woodwork — anywhere the bead should disappear into a painted surface, acrylic latex is the correct product and silicone is simply wrong.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>It tools beautifully.</strong> A wet finger or a tool smooths a latex bead into a clean concave line, and mistakes wipe off with a damp cloth. For anyone caulking for the first time, this forgiveness is worth a great deal — a tidy latex bead usually looks better than a rushed silicone one.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>It bonds to porous materials.</strong> Wood, drywall, plaster, brick and painted surfaces all suit latex, and on those substrates the adhesion difference against silicone narrows considerably.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>It can be recaulked over.</strong> Old latex accepts a fresh bead after cleaning, which makes maintenance simple in a way silicone never is.
        </p>

        <p>
          The limits: it shrinks as the water leaves it, so deep joints need backer rod rather than more caulk; it stiffens and eventually cracks, particularly in sunlight and cold; and in constant wet it will eventually fail. Outdoors, five to ten years is a realistic expectation against silicone's twenty-plus.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Where Latex Genuinely Beats Silicone</h3>
        <p>
          It is worth being precise about this rather than treating latex as the budget option. On interior trim, latex is not a compromise — it is the right product, and silicone there would be a mistake even if it were free. A painted baseboard joint moves very little, never gets wet, and has to vanish under paint; latex delivers all three and silicone fails the only requirement that matters.
        </p>
        <p>
          The same applies to the small gaps left after fitting architrave, filling the line where two painted surfaces meet, or tidying a drywall corner before decorating. These are cosmetic filling jobs rather than sealing jobs, and reaching for a premium sealant is spending money to make the work harder and the finish worse.
        </p>

        <h2 id="hybrids" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          5. Hybrids and Siliconised Acrylic
        </h2>

        <p>
          The shelf muddies this comparison deliberately, and two categories are worth separating.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Siliconised acrylic</strong> — sold as "acrylic latex plus silicone" — is latex with a small silicone content for improved adhesion and water resistance. It is still latex: paintable, water-cleanable, and modestly better than the plain version. It is not a substitute for silicone in a shower or a moving joint, and treating it as one is a common and disappointing mistake.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Hybrid polymer caulks</strong> — variously labelled MS polymer, elastomeric, or advanced polymer — are a genuinely different chemistry. They combine much of silicone's flexibility and longevity with the ability to take paint, they bond to a very wide range of substrates, and the better ones carry long warranties. They cost more per tube and, for most exterior work where the joint has to be painted and also has to move, they are the technically correct answer.
        </p>

        <p>
          If the price difference does not matter to you, a quality hybrid removes most of the trade-offs on this page. Our <a href="/best-exterior-caulk/">exterior caulk reviews</a> cover the products in each category and how they held up in testing.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">How to Read the Label</h3>
        <p>
          Packaging works hard to blur these categories, and three things on a tube tell you more than the marketing does.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>The cleanup instruction.</strong> "Soap and water cleanup" means water-based — latex or a siliconised acrylic, whatever else the front of the tube claims. "Clean up with mineral spirits" means silicone or a solvent-borne product. This is the fastest way to identify what is actually in your hand.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>The movement rating.</strong> Better tubes state a percentage of joint movement the cured bead tolerates. Anything around 25 per cent or above is a genuinely flexible sealant; single-digit figures indicate a filler that will crack in a joint that moves. Plain acrylic latex usually does not publish a figure at all, which is itself informative.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>The warranty.</strong> A tube claiming a 50-year life is a silicone or a hybrid; a 10-year claim is usually latex. The number is marketing, but the relative ordering across a shelf is a reliable proxy for the chemistry inside.
        </p>

        <h2 id="by-job" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          6. Which One, By Job
        </h2>

        <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
          <table class="w-full text-left">
            <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
              <tr>
                <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">The Job</th>
                <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Use</th>
                <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Why</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Shower and bath perimeter</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Silicone with mildewcide</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Constant wet, non-porous, flexes</td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Interior trim and baseboards</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Acrylic latex</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Must be painted, low movement</td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Exterior window and door trim</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Hybrid polymer</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Paintable and handles movement</td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Glass to frame</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Silicone</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Only silicone sticks to glass</td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Kitchen worktop to splashback</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Silicone</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Wet, non-porous, unpainted</td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Siding penetrations and vents</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Hybrid or silicone</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Weather exposure and movement</td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Drywall gaps before painting</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Acrylic latex</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Paint has to cover it</td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="bg-gray-50">
                <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Gutter seams and metal flashing</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Silicone or hybrid</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Metal, weather, thermal movement</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>

        <p>
          Temperature at application matters too, and it catches people out on exterior work. Most caulks want the surface above roughly 40°F, and latex in particular can freeze before it cures if a cold night follows an afternoon application. Silicone and hybrids tolerate cold application better, which is another reason they suit late-season exterior jobs.
        </p>

        <p>
          One case worth calling out separately: joints between two <strong>dissimilar materials</strong> — brick to timber, metal to masonry — move against each other far more than a joint within a single material, because the two sides expand at different rates. Those are silicone or hybrid joints regardless of how much you would prefer to paint them.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Working With Each One</h3>
        <p>
          The application differences are as practical as the performance ones, and they change how long a job takes.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Latex</strong> is forgiving. Cut the nozzle small, run the bead, smooth it with a wet finger or a tool, and wipe any smear away with a damp cloth. Mistakes are recoverable right up until it skins over, which takes several minutes. This is why a first-time caulker produces a better-looking result in latex than in silicone.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Silicone</strong> is not forgiving. It skins quickly, it does not accept a second pass once it has started to set, and anything smeared onto an adjacent surface has to be removed with solvent before it cures — a wet cloth simply spreads it. Work in short runs, tool immediately, and mask both sides of a visible joint if the finish matters. Ventilate too: acetoxy-cure silicone, the common type, releases acetic acid as it cures, which is the vinegar smell.
        </p>
        <p>
          Both want a clean, dry, sound surface. Caulk of any chemistry adheres to what it touches, so a bead run over dust, old flaking sealant or a damp substrate fails regardless of what the tube cost.
        </p>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="common-mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">Common Mistakes</h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Painting Over Silicone</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Paint will not stick to it, no matter the primer. If you have already siliconed a joint that needs painting, the bead has to be cut out entirely — there is no way to rescue it in place.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using Latex in a Shower</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                It will not hold on tile and glass, and constant wet breaks it down. The mould appearing along a bath seal within a year is usually latex that should have been silicone.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Recaulking Over Old Silicone</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Nothing bonds to cured silicone, including new silicone. Cut it out, scrape the residue, clean with a solvent or silicone remover, and only then apply a fresh bead.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Filling a Deep Gap With Caulk Alone</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Latex shrinks as it dries and deep beads of any type cannot flex properly. Anything more than about a quarter inch deep wants backer rod first, with the caulk forming a shallow bead across it.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming Siliconised Acrylic Is Silicone</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                It is latex with a little silicone in it — better than plain latex, nowhere near actual silicone in a wet or moving joint. The name sells more than the chemistry delivers.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Verdict -->
        <div id="verdict" class="my-16 bg-gradient-to-br from-gray-950 to-gray-900 rounded-3xl p-8 lg:p-12 text-white shadow-2xl relative overflow-hidden">
          <div class="absolute top-0 right-0 bg-[#FF4500] text-white font-black text-xs uppercase tracking-widest px-4 py-2 rounded-bl-xl">
            ★ Our Verdict
          </div>
          <h2 id="who-wins" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-white mt-0 mb-6 border-none">Silicone vs Latex: Who Wins?</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6 text-gray-300">
            <p>
              <strong class="text-[#38BDF8]">Choose silicone if:</strong> the joint will not be painted, gets wet, moves, or involves glass, tile, metal or plastic. It lasts decades, stays flexible, and is genuinely waterproof — and it is the only right answer in a bathroom.
            </p>
            <p>
              <strong class="text-[#FF4500]">Choose acrylic latex if:</strong> the joint will be painted. Trim, baseboards, drywall gaps and painted woodwork all want a bead that disappears under paint, tools easily and can be recaulked later without cutting anything out.
            </p>
            <p class="text-lg border-t border-gray-700 pt-6 mt-6">
              <strong class="text-white">The Honest Bottom Line:</strong> Silicone is the better sealant; latex is the better filler. If a joint needs to be both — painted and weatherproof and moving, which describes most exterior trim — skip the argument entirely and buy a hybrid polymer. It costs more per tube than either, and on the joints where this comparison is genuinely difficult it removes the compromise.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <h2 id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          Frequently Asked Questions
        </h2>

        <div class="space-y-6">
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the difference between silicone and latex caulk?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">Silicone stays permanently flexible, sticks to non-porous surfaces like glass, tile and metal, and is waterproof — but it cannot be painted. Acrylic latex tools smoothly, cleans up with water and takes paint perfectly, but it is stiffer, shrinks as it cures, and has a shorter life in wet or high-movement joints.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you paint over silicone?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">No. Paint beads up on cured silicone and peels within weeks, and no primer changes that. Joints that need painting want acrylic latex or a paintable hybrid — products sold as "paintable silicone" are hybrids rather than true silicone.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Which lasts longer?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">Silicone, comfortably — 20 years or more while staying flexible, against 5 to 10 years for acrylic latex outdoors before it hardens and cracks. Hybrid polymers sit between, often rated for 15 years or more while still accepting paint.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Which is best for bathrooms?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">Silicone with a mildewcide. Wet areas need permanent water resistance and adhesion to tile, glass and acrylic, which latex cannot deliver. The only latex joint in a bathroom is one that has to be painted, such as trim meeting a wall.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you put latex over silicone?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">No. Nothing bonds reliably to cured silicone, including fresh silicone. Cut and scrape the old bead out completely and remove residue with a solvent or dedicated silicone remover before applying anything new.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Does latex caulk shrink?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">Yes — it is water-based, so the bead loses volume as it dries, noticeably in deep or wide joints. That is why latex beads often show a hairline gap along one edge after a season, and why deep gaps need backer rod rather than more caulk.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">What is siliconised acrylic?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">Acrylic latex with a little silicone added for better adhesion and water resistance. It stays paintable and water-cleanable and performs somewhat better than plain latex — but it is not silicone, and it is not a substitute in a shower or a high-movement joint.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Which is better outdoors?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">It depends on paint. Unpainted joints around windows, siding penetrations and dissimilar materials favour silicone or a hybrid for movement and longevity. Joints that get painted as part of a trim scheme want acrylic latex or a paintable hybrid, accepting the shorter service life.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

