        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Ask whether the joint gets painted. <strong class="text-white">Painted means latex</strong>, because nothing sticks to silicone. <strong class="text-white">Unpainted, wet or moving means silicone</strong>, because it stays flexible for decades and bonds to the slick surfaces latex cannot hold.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed">
            For the awkward middle — exterior trim that needs paint and also needs to move through the seasons — a hybrid polymer costs a few dollars more per tube and does both jobs properly. And whatever you choose, use backer rod in deep joints: a shallow bead across a rod outlives a deep bead of any chemistry, because it can stretch instead of tearing itself apart as the joint moves.
          </p>
        </div>

